WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.400
2, 1

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.598
On November 18, 2023, Starship&nbsp;
took one step closer to Mars.

00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:10.001
We have liftoff

00:00:10.001 --> 00:00:15.600
One day, Elon Musk expects the most powerful&nbsp;
ever to carry 100 people to the red planet.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.788
Unlike the previous test flight in April, this flight&nbsp;
achieved a milestone: Starship fired its engines

00:00:22.788 --> 00:00:26.708
before detaching from the Super Heavy booster. 
 
Stage separation

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:30.840
This complicated maneuver didn’t happen&nbsp;
previously, as a fuel leak led to a fire

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.320
in the booster, damaging many of its&nbsp;
33 engines. Starship veered off course,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.760
and the team had to destroy it&nbsp;
over water for safety reasons.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.640
And remember the damage to&nbsp;
the launch pad last time?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:49.440
This time, SpaceX reinforced the launch pad with&nbsp;
a steel plate cooled by 260,000 gallons of water.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.360
The plan was to lift off from Starbase in Boca&nbsp;
Chica Texas with the booster splashing down

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.400
close to shore while Starship soared before&nbsp;
landing in the Pacific Ocean near Hawaii.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.020
However, as Elon is aware, not&nbsp;
everything goes according to plan.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:09.640
Clip As you can see the super heavy booster has&nbsp;
just experienced a rapid unscheduled disassembly.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.800
The booster exploded in spectacular fashion&nbsp;
after separating from Starship which is

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.200
believed to have self-destructed after the&nbsp;
team lost contact with it ten minutes in.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.200
SpaceX says the lessons learned from this&nbsp;
flight will help improve Starship’s reliability.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.240
After all, it took the Falcon&nbsp;
1 four tries before it reached

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.160
orbit and saved the company from impending death.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.440
SpaceX even compiled a video showing all of its&nbsp;
failed attempts to land its orbital boosters.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.560
If Mars were not the eventual target,&nbsp;
Elon would not need such a large rocket

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.000
that towers over the SLS and the&nbsp;
Saturn V that took us to the moon.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.560
It’s more than twice the thrust of the Saturn V&nbsp;
- that’s the largest rocket to ever get to orbit.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.080
He had already achieved remarkable success with&nbsp;
the Falcon 9 which has become the world’s most

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:59.440
reliable rocket, regularly transporting crew&nbsp;
and cargo to the ISS, a lucrative business.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.160
But wealth was never the end game.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.680
As Elon expressed in his biography&nbsp;
written by Walter Isaacson,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.320
“I could have made a lot of money, but I&nbsp;
could not have made life multi-planetary.”

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.160
The Falcon 9 is not big enough or powerful&nbsp;
enough to go all the way to Mars. Not even

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.000
its larger sibling, the Falcon Heavy, can do that.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.680
The Falcons are also not fully reusable.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.240
While the first stage returns to Earth,&nbsp;
and the payload fairings are fished out

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:29.320
of the sea, the second stage is not recovered. 
 
Starship is designed as a fully reusable rocket,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.920
essential for long-duration missions, a capability&nbsp;
enhanced by its stainless steel construction.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.120
Like the kind used on the Cybertruck.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.200
Elon fell in love with stainless steel&nbsp;
a few years back, joking to his team,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.240
“Stainless steel and I should&nbsp;
go get a room somewhere.”

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.760
Starship was originally supposed&nbsp;
to be made out of carbon fiber.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.720
However, the carbon fiber sheets&nbsp;
were expensive and not up to the job.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.040
Even the specialized expertise that went into them&nbsp;
could not prevent issues like developing wrinkles.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.920
Elon knew that if they went&nbsp;
the carbon fiber route:

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.240
“This extrapolates to death. I’ll never&nbsp;
be able to get to Mars,” he told Isaacson.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.600
So, inspired by the earliest Atlas rockets&nbsp;
that took the first Americans to orbit,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.960
he opted for stainless steel.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.680
It was an odd move according to his executive team

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.880
because stainless steel is&nbsp;
heavier than carbon fiber.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.600
But Elon looked at the situation differently.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.840
The high melting point of stainless&nbsp;
steel eliminated the need for as

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.200
many heat shields to protect Starship as it&nbsp;
re-enters the atmosphere…where temperatures

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:34.040
can soar as high as 1,500 degrees&nbsp;
Celsius, or 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.720
Fewer heat shields mean the&nbsp;
overall weight is reduced.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.200
And stainless steel maintains its strength even

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.800
when holding supercooled liquid oxygen and&nbsp;
methane which is used to fuel Starship.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.600
The first prototype of Starship was the Starhopper

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.880
which had retractable legs to test how&nbsp;
it could land when it returns to earth.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.520
However, Elon has since decided to&nbsp;
try and to catch Starship - both the

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.680
ship and the booster - with the tower&nbsp;
that holds the rocket on the launchpad.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.640
The SpaceX team was, once again,&nbsp;
nervous at such a bold proposal:

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.280
What happens if the catch fails and destroys&nbsp;
the launch tower and the next flight is delayed?

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.160
When someone on X asked why not just use the&nbsp;
landing legs for the booster, Elon replied:

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:21.000
“Legs would certainly work, but best&nbsp;
part is no part, best step is no step”

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.200
Highlighting his preference for&nbsp;
reducing complexity to bring down

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.320
costs and make space travel more affordable.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:32.120
He’s applied the same principle to the design&nbsp;
of the Raptor engines that power Starship.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.960
He wanted to lower the cost&nbsp;
of a single engine prototype

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.720
from $2 million to $200,000, cutting costs by 90%.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.120
This strategy worked for him in the past&nbsp;
when he wanted to purchase refurbished

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.400
ICBMs from Russia but found the cost&nbsp;
of $8 million for one too high and

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.320
decided to create his own company&nbsp;
to build more affordable rockets.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.360
He’s since coined the term “idiot&nbsp;
index” - a metric comparing the cost

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:00.080
of a component to the cost of its raw materials. 
 
Let’s say the nozzle for the engine hypothetically

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:07.760
costs $20,000 but the raw material cost is $2,000,&nbsp;
the ratio on Elon’s “idiot index” would be 10:1.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.320
A high ratio suggests the&nbsp;
production process for this

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.640
component is more expensive&nbsp;
than it really needs to be.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.040
When Elon asked financial analyst&nbsp;
Lucas Hughes “What are the best

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.880
parts in Raptor as judged by the idiot index?”

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.520
And Hughes responded, “I’m not sure.”

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.640
Elon reamed him out. As reported&nbsp;
in Isaacson’s book, he said:

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.920
“You better be fucking sure in the future&nbsp;
you know these things off the top of your

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.920
head. If you ever come into a meeting&nbsp;
and do not know what are the idiot parts,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.000
then your resignation will&nbsp;
be accepted immediately.”

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.600
Unbeknownst to Elon then, the&nbsp;
analyst had other things on his mind.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.640
He had just lost a newborn&nbsp;
daughter a few weeks before.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.040
Elon had suffered a similar loss when&nbsp;
his own firstborn, Nevada Alexander,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.840
died of sudden infant death syndrome.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.560
When Isaacson later asked Elon if he&nbsp;
felt he was being too harsh, Elon said:

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.840
“I try to criticize the action, not the person.&nbsp;
We all make mistakes. What matters is whether a

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:07.040
person has a good feedback loop, can seek&nbsp;
criticism from others, and can improve.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.640
Physics does not care about hurt feelings. It&nbsp;
cares about whether you got the rocket right.”

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.760
The analyst was much more prepared the next day.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.840
He discovered that parts with the highest cost

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.800
compared to raw material required&nbsp;
a lot of high-precision machining.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.640
Elon decided that the person in charge of

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.640
each component should try to&nbsp;
get their costs down by 80%.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.000
And if they couldn’t, they should “...step&nbsp;
aside if someone else might be able to do so.”

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.160
Elon has always operated with an extreme&nbsp;
sense of urgency as if every second counts,

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.400
declaring, “We need to get to Mars before I die.”

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.760
He assigned the responsibility for revamping&nbsp;
Raptor to a young engineer named Jacob McKenzie.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.560
The directive, in Musk’s words, was to “...delete&nbsp;
any part that is even *potentially* unnecessary.”

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.240
McKenzie consulted a Tesla executive to&nbsp;
help streamline SpaceX's manufacturing,

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.560
drawing on how Tesla continuously streamlines&nbsp;
its process of mass-producing electric vehicles.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.160
The result is that version 2 of the Raptor engine

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.440
removed many of the intricate plumbing&nbsp;
components and looked much simpler.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.000
V1 looks kinda like a Christmas tree,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.440
spaghetti pile. A lot of fiddly&nbsp;
bits. Raptor 2 costs about half

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:21.280
as much as Raptor 1 despite having much more&nbsp;
thrust…it’s only going to get better from here.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.920
Elon’s hands-on approach is embodied by his&nbsp;
choice to live just a mile away from Starbase.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:31.280
Despite being the richest person in the&nbsp;
world, he resides in a two-bedroom house,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.640
where caffeine-free Diet Cokes are common, as&nbsp;
are midnight meetings with the Starship team.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.800
Living and breathing the mission&nbsp;
to make life multi-planetary.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.280
Building the most advanced rockets in the world

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.200
would not be possible without the&nbsp;
hard work of brilliant engineers.

